### RAF CONSULT MARS — Sprint 1 HTTP smoke flow (US01 + US18/US19)
#
# Run this file in a client with a cookie jar. The JetBrains HTTP Client keeps
# cookies per host automatically; in other clients enable/persist cookies. The
# access token is httpOnly: there is no bearer header, and the login/reset/change
# response bodies never contain a token.
#
# Flow: login -> me -> forgot-password -> reset-password -> login (new password)
#       -> change-password -> me -> logout. The reset revokes every session, so the
#       cookie from step 1 is dead by step 5: log in again with the new password.
#
# Local API: `npm run start:dev` in backend/app (http://localhost:4000).
# Reset email: with EMAIL_TRANSPORT=smtp and Mailtrap configured (see
# backend/app/.env.example), open the Mailtrap inbox, open the
# "Reset your RAF CONSULT MARS password" message, copy the raw token from the
# ?token= query parameter of the reset link and paste it into @resetToken below.
# With EMAIL_TRANSPORT=memory no mail leaves the process — use Mailtrap for manual runs.
# NEVER commit a real token or JWT: this file is a template, not a secret store.
#
# Every non-GET request requires `X-MARS-CSRF: 1`. Browser code must also send
# `credentials: 'include'`; see docs/api-contract-us18-us19.md §6.

@baseUrl = http://localhost:4000
@csrf = 1
@email = pm@rafconsult.test
@password = RafConsult!2026
@newPassword = A brand new passphrase 2026
@changedPassword = Another rotated passphrase 2026
# Paste the 43-character raw token from the forgot-password email (base64url).
@resetToken = paste-raw-token-from-reset-email-here

### 1. login — sets the httpOnly mars_at cookie; no token in the body
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json
X-MARS-CSRF: {{csrf}}

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### 2. me — proves the cookie jar is authenticated
GET {{baseUrl}}/auth/me

### 3. forgot-password — always 204 for a valid email (known, unknown, inactive, invited)
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/forgot-password
Content-Type: application/json
X-MARS-CSRF: {{csrf}}

{
  "email": "{{email}}"
}

### 4. reset-password — 200 with an empty body, no Set-Cookie (no auto-login)
# Any expired/used/unknown/inactive link returns the same generic 400:
#   {"message":"This password reset link is invalid or has expired."}
# A policy failure returns 400 with the policy message and does not consume the link.
# Copy the Mailtrap token into @resetToken before sending this request.
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/reset-password
Content-Type: application/json
X-MARS-CSRF: {{csrf}}

{
  "token": "{{resetToken}}",
  "newPassword": "{{newPassword}}"
}

### 5. login again — the reset revoked every session, so step 1's cookie is dead
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json
X-MARS-CSRF: {{csrf}}

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{newPassword}}"
}

### 6. change-password — rotates mars_at; 200 {user, expiresIn}, no token in the body
# Wrong currentPassword -> 401 {"message":"Invalid email or password"}; the
# previous cookie and a second device's cookie are rejected afterwards.
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/change-password
Content-Type: application/json
X-MARS-CSRF: {{csrf}}

{
  "currentPassword": "{{newPassword}}",
  "newPassword": "{{changedPassword}}"
}

### 7. me — the rotated cookie works
GET {{baseUrl}}/auth/me

### 8. logout — 204 and clears the cookie; the rotated cookie is dead afterwards
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/logout
X-MARS-CSRF: {{csrf}}

### 9. Negative: login with the old password after step 6 — expect 401
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json
X-MARS-CSRF: {{csrf}}

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### 10. Negative: forgot-password without the CSRF header — expect 403
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/forgot-password
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}"
}
